= What the asymmetry explains <sec-explains>

The dictionary of @sec-dict leaves one puzzle open: on the $GG_m$ side the *rank* is free and the
*class number* is hard, while on the $E$ side the *rank* is the hard thing. @sec-compact answers
it, and the answer is the same fact from the other direction.

#block(stroke: 0.6pt + black, inset: 9pt, radius: 3pt, width: 100%)[
  A group whose archimedean avatar is *non-compact* has its rank pinned down by that avatar: the
  lattice lives in a Euclidean space whose dimension is visible in advance, and the only question
  is whether it fills it. A group whose archimedean avatar is *compact* has no such ambient space,
  and no archimedean invariant to read the rank off.
]

#v(2mm)
That line is where it should be. For an algebraic *torus* $T$ the rank of the $S$-unit group
$T(cal(O)_S)$ is still given by a formula in the Galois module of characters and the decomposition
groups at the places of $S$ (Ono, @sec-refs [6]) --- Dirichlet's theorem is the case
$T = GG_m$. For an abelian variety no such formula is known or expected. For a *semiabelian*
variety $1 --> T --> G --> A --> 0$ over a number field, finite generation of $G(K)$ is one
theorem covering both cases, and the rank splits into a computable toric part and an unknown
abelian part. The dividing line inside that one theorem is exactly the dividing line of
@sec-compact.

#block(fill: rgb("#fff4e6"), inset: 8pt, radius: 3pt, width: 100%)[
  *A remark on the other side of the ledger.* The class number formula puts $h$ and $R$ on the same
  side of one equation, so what one field spends on the regulator another spends on the class
  number --- `mersenne-fermat-class.typ` §4.1 watches exactly that happen at Mersenne
  discriminants, where $QQ(sqrt(-M_p))$ spends its arithmetic on $h$ and $QQ(sqrt(+M_p))$ spends
  nearly all of it on $R$. Nothing above says the $h$ side has a formula for *its* size. What it
  says is that the $R$ side is the side whose *rank* has one, and that this is an archimedean
  accident rather than a structural advantage.
]

= What the companion script checks <sec-gp>

`dirichlet-mordell-weil.gp`, results in `results/dirichlet-mordell-weil.txt`.

#v(1mm)
- *(1)* The identity of @sec-why: on twelve fundamental units across six signatures, the
  archimedean log vector sums to zero, $abs(N(u)) = 1$, and
  $(1 slash n) sum n_v log^+ abs(u)_v = norm(lambda(u))_1 slash 2n$. Zero discrepancies.
- *(2)* Northcott made exact: $h(epsilon) = R slash 2$ and
  $\#{u : h(u) <= B} = 2(2 floor(2B slash R) + 1)$ in three real quadratic fields at three bounds
  each. Zero mismatches.
- *(3)* Step (b) of @sec-realsplit: rank $= r_1 + r_2 - 1$ on nine fields, and the covolume of the
  log lattice recomputed from the embeddings equals `bnfinit`'s regulator.
- *(4)* The contrast of @sec-compact: $min_(n <= N) abs(log abs(epsilon^n))$ is constant in $N$,
  while $min_(n <= N) norm(n z slash omega)$ on `43a1` stays below $1 slash N$ at every $N$ up to
  $10^5$.
- *(5)* The contraction rates of @sec-descent: $h(epsilon^m) slash h(epsilon) = m$ against
  $hat(h)(m P) slash hat(h)(P) = m^2$, for $m = 2, ..., 6$.

